\section{Judging a statement by its own precision}{

\code{tol = "decimals"} reads the tolerance off the filing. XBRL states the
precision of every fact: \code{decimals="-3"} means accurate to thousands, so such
a figure carries up to 500 kroner of rounding, and a step of several adds
theirs together.

The failures are concentrated where you would expect: of the 63 filings
whose cascade leaves a step unsolved or unexplained at a one-krone
tolerance, half report at \code{-3} or coarser, against 6\% of the 3,857 that
settle every step.

As the default it does harm. Run on every statement in a 4,304-filing
corpus, a tolerance read off the filing settles 17 more steps and unsettles
16: with more combinations fitting, the first step no longer pins the entry
concept, and steps that were verified become \code{"given"}. So the default
stays at one krone, and the filing's own precision is offered as a fallback
(see the next section).
}

\section{Looser readings, only where the strict one fails}{

A statement the strict reading leaves unsettled is offered three more, in
order. Each is kept only if it settles more steps and every step that was
unique stays unique, and \code{pass} names the reading that settled it:
\itemize{
\item \code{"retried"} -- a line a step did not need goes to the step below rather
than being consumed.
\item \code{"decimals"} -- the filing's own precision as the tolerance.
\item \code{"two left out"} -- that, with one more line allowed to fall out of a
step.
}

Over the corpus the strict reading settles every step in 4,234 filings, and
the three readings take that to 4,257, with no unique step lost. The
solutions with two lines left out take the taxonomy's debit/credit direction
on all 72 of the lines they use, but leaving lines out is still the weakest
of the claims, and \code{pass} lets it be filtered out.
}

\section{Why step by step}{

Reconciling the whole statement at once fails in both directions. It fails
to solve, because twenty items against a bottom line rarely have one
answer; and worse, it sometimes \emph{appears} to solve by mixing leaf items,
their parents and the running subtotals into a combination that happens to
reach the total. Four or five items against one subtotal is decidable, and
when a step does not reconcile you know which one.
}

\section{The reporting boundary is not a gap}{

Accounting class B may present a gross figure instead of turnover and its
components, and nearly all do -- 4,172 of 4,304 filings enter at
\code{Bruttofortjeneste/Bruttotab} or \code{Bruttoresultat}, and three at turnover.
The taxonomy says so itself: the trading group has two exits, \code{Bruttoresultat}
and \code{Bruttofortjeneste/Bruttotab}. What varies is therefore which income
concept a filing \emph{enters} on, not whether it is complete.

A filing entering at a gross figure has nothing to reconcile in the first
step -- the concept is given, not derived -- and its figure is carried into
the second. Reconciling the remaining steps is a complete result for that
filing, and \code{entry} records where it started.
}

\section{How the steps are found}{

From the presentation hierarchy, not from a list of element names. The
groups are the statement's abstract top-level nodes; within each, the
itemised lines are the ones that are themselves broken down, and the
subtotal is what follows them. The final step is the statement's flat tail,
ending at the last line the filing reports -- which is the result for the
year, or the result after minority interests where a filing gives one.
}

